# SvelteKit 2 Core

Cursor: SvelteKit 2 (December 2023, with `$app/state` added in 2.12, ongoing minor releases through 2.60.1) introduced breaking changes to `error()`, `redirect()`, `cookies`, `goto()`, top-level `load` promises, and the `$app/stores` -> `$app/state` migration. Target: `@sveltejs/kit ^2.60.1`.

The companion `sveltekit-anti-patterns` rule rejects the SvelteKit 1 shapes (entries #17, #18, #19, #20, #21, #22, #23, #26). This rule explains the positive form.

## `error()` and `redirect()` no longer thrown by you

SvelteKit 2 made `error()` and `redirect()` throw internally. Drop `throw`. The functions never return so TypeScript can narrow types after them. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#redirect-and-error-are-no-longer-thrown-by-you

```ts
import { error, redirect } from '@sveltejs/kit'
import type { PageServerLoad } from './$types'

export const load: PageServerLoad = async ({ params, locals }) => {
  const post = await locals.db.posts.findById(params.id)
  if (!post) error(404, 'Post not found')   // throws Error internally
  if (!locals.user) redirect(303, '/login')  // throws Redirect internally
  return { post }
}
```

If you wrap in `try/catch`, re-throw redirects and errors:

```ts
import { error, redirect, isRedirect, isHttpError } from '@sveltejs/kit'

try {
  redirect(303, '/login')
} catch (e) {
  if (isRedirect(e) || isHttpError(e)) throw e
  // handle other failures
}
```

## `cookies.set` / `cookies.delete` require `path`

SvelteKit 2 removed the implicit `path: '/'` default. Set it explicitly. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#path-is-required-when-setting-cookies

```ts
cookies.set('session', token, {
  path: '/',
  httpOnly: true,
  secure: true,
  sameSite: 'lax',
  maxAge: 60 * 60 * 24 * 7,
})

cookies.delete('session', { path: '/' })
```

## Top-level promises in `load` are no longer auto-awaited

SvelteKit 1 auto-awaited promises returned at the top level of `load`. SvelteKit 2 surfaces them as `Promise<T>` in your data. Use `Promise.all` for parallel fetches. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#top-level-promises-are-no-longer-awaited

```ts
import type { PageLoad } from './$types'

export const load: PageLoad = async ({ fetch }) => {
  const [posts, tags] = await Promise.all([
    fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('/api/tags').then(r => r.json()),
  ])
  return { posts, tags }
}
```

If you genuinely want streaming, return the unawaited promise and consume it with `{#await}`:

```ts
export const load: PageLoad = async ({ fetch }) => {
  return {
    user: await fetch('/api/user').then(r => r.json()),
    posts: fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json()),  // streamed
  }
}
```

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  let { data } = $props()
</script>
<p>Hello {data.user.name}</p>
{#await data.posts}
  <p>Loading posts.</p>
{:then posts}
  <ul>{#each posts as p}<li>{p.title}</li>{/each}</ul>
{/await}
```

## `goto()` rejects external URLs

`goto()` is for in-app, client-side navigation. External URLs throw. Use `window.location.href`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#goto-no-longer-accepts-external-urls

```ts
import { goto } from '$app/navigation'

goto('/dashboard')                    // GOOD
goto('/posts/123', { replaceState: true })  // GOOD
window.location.href = 'https://stripe.example/checkout/abc'  // GOOD external
```

## `$app/state` replaces `$app/stores` (2.12+)

`$app/state` exposes `page`, `navigating`, `updated` as plain reactive objects (no `$` prefix, no store subscription). Works inside and outside `<script>` blocks. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/$app-state

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { page, navigating, updated } from '$app/state'
</script>

<p>Path: {page.url.pathname}</p>
<p>Param: {page.params.slug}</p>
{#if navigating.type}<p>Navigating.</p>{/if}
{#if updated.current}<button onclick={() => location.reload()}>Update available</button>{/if}
```

The Svelte 4 store form (`$page.url.pathname`) still works for backward compatibility but is deprecated for new code.

## `resolvePath` renamed `resolveRoute`

```ts
import { resolveRoute } from '$app/paths'
const href = resolveRoute('/posts/[slug]', { slug: 'hello' })
```

## `paths.relative` default flipped to `true`

`svelte.config.js`:

```js
const config = {
  kit: {
    paths: { relative: true },  // SvelteKit 2 default
  },
}
export default config
```

Set explicitly if your code depends on absolute paths.

## `reroute` hook for URL aliasing

The `reroute` hook (added in `@sveltejs/kit` 2.3) maps an incoming `URL` to a different SvelteKit route without an HTTP redirect. Useful for i18n, A/B routing, legacy path aliases. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/hooks#Universal-hooks-reroute

```ts
// src/hooks.ts (universal, runs on server AND client)
import type { Reroute } from '@sveltejs/kit'

export const reroute: Reroute = ({ url }) => {
  if (url.pathname === '/about-us') return '/about'
  if (url.pathname.startsWith('/blog/')) {
    return url.pathname.replace('/blog/', '/posts/')
  }
}
```

## Shallow routing via `pushState` / `replaceState`

For modal flows that should update the URL without a full navigation. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/shallow-routing

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { pushState } from '$app/navigation'
  import { page } from '$app/state'

  function openModal(id: string) {
    pushState(`?modal=${id}`, { modal: id })
  }
</script>

{#if page.state.modal}
  <Dialog onclose={() => history.back()} />
{/if}
```

## Why these matter for LLM output

Pre-2024 trained models will produce:

- `throw error(...)` / `throw redirect(...)` (anti-pattern #17)
- `cookies.set('x', v, { httpOnly: true })` without `path` (anti-pattern #19)
- Top-level unawaited promises returned from `load` (anti-pattern #20)
- `$page` from `$app/stores` instead of `page` from `$app/state` (anti-pattern #22)
- `resolvePath` instead of `resolveRoute` (anti-pattern #23)
